WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.500
Cameraman: Alright, Michael.

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:02.500
You’ve got 60 seconds.

00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:05.230
Michael: I’ll speak really fast and we’ll
get it all in…

00:00:05.230 --> 00:00:10.290
Neither the media delegate counters nor the
candidate delegate counters are going to wait

00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:15.830
between June 7 and July 18 to see how many
delegates show up at the convention and support

00:00:15.830 --> 00:00:16.830
different candidates.

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:22.600
We will know after the June 7 final primaries
whether or not Donald Trump has won in primaries

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:27.300
or caucuses 1237 votes or if he’s visibly
short of that number.

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:33.870
Trump has suggested that this is some kind
of arbitrary number—actually, it’s a majority.

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:39.410
If Trump does not get to 1237—if he’s
visibly short of it—I think that the convention

00:00:39.410 --> 00:00:44.469
will move to other candidates, and I think
by far the likeliest nominee in that situation

00:00:44.469 --> 00:00:46.329
is Ted Cruz.

00:00:46.329 --> 00:00:52.290
What is John Kasich’s strategy to get nominated
at Cleveland in his home state of Ohio?

00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:58.420
I think the one problem with Kasich’s apparent
strategy is that always it relies on, as one

00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:02.850
essential step, the other guy messing up.

00:01:02.850 --> 00:01:08.610
You can’t always rely on that happening.

